<problem><title>Video review questions</title>
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>An differential equation of the form
  <me>\frac{dy}{dt} = f(y)</me>
  is called an <term>autonomous differential equation</term> because...</p>
  <answer name="ans1a">
    <choice>it is self-governing</choice>
    <choice>it states that the derivative, <m>\frac{dy}{dt}</m>, only depends on time and is not a function of <m>y</m></choice>
    <choice credit="1">it states that the derivative, <m>\frac{dy}{dt}</m>, only depends on the function <m>y</m> and is not explicitly a function of time, <m>t</m>.</choice>
  </answer>

  <feedback condition="$_choice2.submitted">If this were the case, we would instead have what is called a <ref to="doenet:7TnsSPfdT3XFW3iGJ1EoWM">pure-time differential equation</ref></feedback>
</exercise>
  
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Consider the <term>autonomous differential equation</term> given by
  <me>\frac{dg}{dt} = 4g</me>
Finding a <term>solution</term> to this differential equation means to</p>
  <answer name="ans1b">
    <choice credit="1">find a function <m>g(t)</m>, whose derivative is equal to 4 times the function itself.</choice>
    <choice>differentiate the function <m>g</m> with respect to time four times</choice>
    <choice>integrate the function <m>g</m> between <m>t = 0</m> and <m>t = 4</m></choice>
  </answer>


<p>We used the "guess and check" method to arrive at 3 possible solutions, where <m>C</m> in each of these functions is an arbitrary constant. Which of the following proposed functions, <m>g(t)</m>, solves the differential equation stated above?</p>
  <answer name="ans1c">
    <choice><m>g(t) = 4t + C</m></choice>
    <choice credit="1"><m>g(t) = Ce^{4t}</m></choice>
    <choice><m>g(t) = t^4 + C</m></choice>
  </answer>

<hint>Perform the "check" part of the "guess and check " method: differentiate <m>g(t)</m> and see if the answer you get is equal to 4 times the original function, <m>g(t)</m></hint>
  
</exercise>
</problem>
  
</section>
